# 0839 operation-allocation observer

This is an isolated evidence binary for the 0839 cached ordered-Part workload.
It uses the same in-memory corpus, source-backed `Parts` route, deterministic
payload/order checks, and execution-resource checks as `../probe/main.rs`.
`cached-part-memory-alloc` accepts `--route parts` only; its output is
allocation evidence and makes no wall-clock, CPU-time, speedup, or regression
claim.

`main.rs` enables the copied `CountingSystemAllocator` before entering the
workload.  The safe workload keeps `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`.  The copied
`allocation_metrics.rs` is the existing operation observer, whose reported
identity is `system_allocator_operation_scoped` with counter revision
`serialized_region_peak_v3`; the allocator identity is
`CountingSystemAllocator(std::alloc::System)`.

For every warmup and measured sample, the region begins immediately before
`SourceBackedPackage::read_parts_ordered` and finishes immediately after the
returned `PartBatch`.  It therefore includes process-wide worker allocations
and the returned batch while it is retained.  Package setup, the optional
verified preload, resource snapshots, byte verification, batch/package drops,
phase handshakes, and report serialization are outside the region.

The allocation and counting-allocator tests use the shared
`allocation_metrics::TEST_LOCK`, so their global counter state cannot contend
when Cargo runs the test suite in parallel.  The root-owned driver remains
responsible for building, testing, capturing, and validating this isolated
manifest.

Unit tests do not install the process-global wrapper: the wrapper tests invoke
its methods directly, while synthetic counter tests inject exact callbacks.
Installing it in those tests also observes test-runner and thread-construction
allocations, invalidating their synthetic exact-count premises. Normal builds
install the global wrapper unchanged; child qualification verifies that runtime
path. The failed initial combined test run is retained in the packet.
